# PKGBUILD patches: use `_patches`

When adding or updating patches in a PKGBUILD, define patch filenames once in
`_patches` and reuse that array in `source` and `prepare()`. Do not list the
same patch file in multiple places.

Reference: `make` commit 3e12c4917 (`make: Fixes building with gcc 15`).

## Pattern

```bash
_patches=(
  0001-fixes-building-with-gcc-15.patch
)

source=("https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/make/${pkgname}-${pkgver}.tar.gz"{,.sig}
        ${_patches[@]})
sha256sums=('dd16fb1d67bfab79a72f5e8390735c49e3e8e70b4945a15ab1f81ddb78658fb3'
            'SKIP'
            '3bedb8aeef4316f4b909e42a3255fd2da4207a1594cc546538d83761efe59ffe')

prepare() {
  cd ${srcdir}/${pkgname}-${pkgver}

  for patch in "${_patches[@]}"; do
    msg2 "Patching with ${patch}"
    patch -Nbp1 -i "${srcdir}/${patch}"
  done
}
```

## Rules

- Name the array `_patches`, not `patches`.
- Put `_patches` before `source=`.
- Append `${_patches[@]}` to `source`; never repeat patch filenames there.
- Add one `sha256sums` entry per patch (same order as `_patches`). Run
  `updpkgsums` in the package directory after adding patches.
- Apply patches in `prepare()` by looping over `"${_patches[@]}"`.
- Use `0001-`, `0002-`, ... prefixes for new patches; keep comments for
  upstream/Fedora origin when helpful (see `zsh/PKGBUILD`).
- Patches not in `_patches` (e.g. one-off `Makefile.in.patch`) stay listed
  separately in `source` and applied on their own in `prepare()`.

## When refactoring an existing PKGBUILD

1. Move all patch filenames from `source=` into `_patches`.
2. Replace the inline list with `${_patches[@]}`.
3. Replace individual `patch -i ...` calls with the loop (unless a patch needs
   a different strip level or flags).
4. Verify `sha256sums` count still matches `source` entries.
